  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "0aea1ebb-0e20-936e-619a-9787e0c5014e",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "8b0df4cb-fbdf-40ab-9f49-28d5679553b1",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Nauman, known for her extensive research in the field, emphasized that moral education in Pakistan is often narrowly focused on religious teachings, with good behavior predominantly interpreted through a religious lens. Her findings highlighted significant disparities in how moral education is approached across different types of schools, including public schools, private institutions, and religious seminaries. She pointed out that these variations often lead to inconsistent moral guidance for students, which is exacerbated by the traditional, teacher-centered classroom environments prevalent in many schools.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "During her presentation, Dr Nauman advocated for a more integrated approach to moral education that combines religious and secular perspectives, emphasising the importance of cognitive skills and the recognition of diversity to prepare students for the complexities of a globalised world. She underscored the critical need for such an approach to be inclusive and comprehensive, highlighting the pivotal role of teachers in fostering ethical development. She also called for effective teacher training to ensure that moral education is balanced and not sidelined, promoting a holistic approach to student development in Pakistan.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
